Description: live terminal UI for troubleshooting Linux servers
 whytop is a process monitor for working out why a machine is behaving badly,
 rather than only what it is doing. Alongside the process table it shows
 per-core CPU, memory, swap, disk and network meters, and a verdict line
 naming the problems it can see: a full filesystem, a failed unit, a process
 the OOM killer recently killed, a mount that has stopped responding.
 .
 Opening a process shows its tree, its open files and sockets, the systemd
 unit that started it, and the tail of that unit's journal, refreshed live.
 Processes can be stopped, killed or restarted, and their unit files edited,
 each behind a confirmation and each recorded in the audit log.
 .
 A remote Linux host can be watched over SSH with --host. Nothing is
 installed on the remote host: whytop reads /proc over an ordinary SSH
 session, and authenticates with keys or ssh-agent only.
 .
 whytop is useful as an unprivileged user; root, or the three capabilities
 named in the manual page, reveal other users' sockets, disk I/O and open
 files.
